{"id": 3330839, "title": "CVE-2025-10148: predictable WebSocket mask", "submitted": "2025-09-08", "source_url": "https://hackerone.com/reports/3330839", "excerpt": "No AI was involved.\n\n## Summary:\nThe curl WebSocket implementation generates a fixed masking key at the beginning of a connection an re-uses it for every frame:\n* Generation of masking key `enc.mask` in `Curl_ws_accept`: https://github.com/curl/curl/blob/455afa1de5182b95a5dcc988f18cdff584b95239/lib/ws.c#L1340\n* Usage in `ws_enc_write_head`: https://github.com/curl/curl/blob/455afa1de5182b95a5dcc988f18cdff584b95239/lib/ws.c#L879\n* Usage in `ws_enc_write_payload`: https://github.com/curl/curl/blob/455afa1de5182b95a5dcc988f18cdff584b95239/lib/ws.c#L930\n\n[RFC-6455 §5.3](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6455#section-5.3) states:\n> [...] The masking key is a 32-bit value chosen at random by the client. When preparing a masked frame, the client MUST pick a fresh masking key from the set of allowed 32-bit values.  The masking key needs to be unpredictable; thus, the masking key MUST be derived from a strong source of entropy, and the masking key for a given frame MUST NOT  make it simple for a server/proxy to predict the masking key for a  subsequent frame.  The unpredictability of the masking key is  essential to prevent authors of malicious applications from selecting  the bytes that appear on the wire.  [...]\n\nBecause the same masking key is repeatedly used for every frame, it is trivial for an attacker to \"guess\" the masking key of all subsequent frames.\n\n## Affected version\nAll versions since WebSocket support was added.\n\n## Steps To Reproduce:\nNone\n\n## Supporting Material/References:\nNone\n\n## Impact\n\nSee \"Security Considerations > Attacks On Infrastructure (Masking)\" in [RFC-6455 §10.3](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6455#section-10.3). Here a short excerpt:\n> [...] As this protocol was being developed, an experiment was conducted to demonstrate a class of attacks on proxies that led to the poisoning of caching proxies deployed in the wild [TALKING].  The general form of the attack was to establish a connection to a server under the \"attacker's\" control, perform an UPGRADE on the HTTP connection similar to what the WebSocket Protocol does to establish a connection, and subsequently send data over that UPGRADEd connection that looked like a GET request for a specific known resource (which in an attack would likely be something like a widely deployed script for tracking hits or a resource on an ad-serving network).  The remote server would resp", "excerpt_truncated": true, "final": "needs_human", "final_label": "Needs human review", "tone": "muted", "explain": "The available evidence does not settle the report. A maintainer should investigate.", "confidence": 0.95, "judge_verdict": "likely_valid", "receipts_override": "judge quoted an external source that does not say that (https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc6455.txt)", "policy_section": "", "grounding": {"verdict": "grounded", "rev": "455afa1de518", "rev_source": "permalink 455afa1de518", "counts": {"grounded": 9, "ungrounded_strong": 0, "ungrounded_medium": 0}, "problems": []}, "summary": "The curl WebSocket implementation reuses a fixed masking key for all frames, violating RFC 6455's requirement for unpredictable per-frame masking.", "claims": [{"id": "C1", "kind": "flaw", "text": "generates a fixed masking key at the beginning of a connection and re-uses it for every frame", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The mask is generated once at connection acceptance (line 1340) and then reused unchanged in both ws_enc_write_head (line 879) and ws_enc_write_payload (line 930) for all frames sent on that connection.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/ws.c", "line": 1340, "note": "Curl_rand generates mask once in Curl_ws_accept", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/ws.c", "line": 879, "note": "ws_enc_write_head copies enc->mask into every frame header", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/ws.c", "line": 930, "note": "ws_enc_write_payload XORs payload with same enc->mask for every frame", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C2", "kind": "location", "text": "masking key generation occurs in Curl_ws_accept at lib/ws.c line 1340", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "Line 1340-1341 in master (line 1028-1029 in curl-8_15_0) shows the masking key generation in Curl_ws_accept.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/ws.c", "line": 1340, "note": "Curl_rand call generating ws->enc.mask", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C3", "kind": "location", "text": "masking key usage occurs in ws_enc_write_head at lib/ws.c line 879", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "Line 879 in master shows the mask being copied into each outgoing frame's header.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/ws.c", "line": 879, "note": "memcpy(&head[hlen], &enc->mask, 4) adds mask to frame header", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C4", "kind": "location", "text": "masking key usage occurs in ws_enc_write_payload at lib/ws.c line 930", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "Line 930 in master shows the same mask being used to XOR each payload byte.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/ws.c", "line": 930, "note": "buf[i] ^ enc->mask[enc->xori] uses mask for payload masking", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C5", "kind": "impact", "text": "trivial for an attacker to guess the masking key of all subsequent frames", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "Since the 32-bit mask is sent in clear in every frame header (line 879) and reused for all payload masking (line 930), an observer who sees one frame learns the mask for all subsequent frames.", "citations": [{"file": "lib/ws.c", "line": 1340, "note": "Single 32-bit mask generated once", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/ws.c", "line": 879, "note": "Same mask sent in every frame header (unmasked)", "valid": true}, {"file": "lib/ws.c", "line": 930, "note": "Same mask used for all payload XOR operations", "valid": true}]}, {"id": "C6", "kind": "reproduction", "text": "None", "status": "supported", "tone": "good", "reasoning": "The report accurately states no reproduction steps were provided.", "citations": []}], "external_checks": [{"statement": "RFC 6455 §5.3 requires a fresh masking key for each frame: 'When preparing a masked frame, the client MUST pick a fresh masking key from the set of allowed 32-bit values. The masking key needs to be unpredictable; thus, the masking key MUST be derived from a strong source of entropy, and the masking key for a given frame MUST NOT make it simple for a server/proxy to predict the masking key for a subsequent frame.'", "status": "confirmed", "url": "https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc6455.txt", "quote": "The masking key is a 32-bit value chosen at random by the client. When preparing a masked frame, the client MUST pick a fresh masking key from the set of allowed 32-bit values. The masking key needs to be unpredictable; thus, the masking key MUST be derived from a strong source of entropy, and the masking key for a given frame MUST NOT make it simple for a server/proxy to predict the masking key for a subsequent frame."}], "external_sources": [{"url": "https://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc6455.txt", "title": "RFC 6455: The WebSocket Protocol", "for": "The masking key is a 32-bit value chosen at random by the client. [...] The masking key needs to be unpredictable; thus, the masking key MUST be derived from a strong source of entropy, and the maskin"}, {"url": "https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/open/n3232/xshtext.pdf", "title": "Introduction", "for": "The masking key is a 32-bit value chosen at random by the client. [...] The masking key needs to be unpredictable; thus, the masking key MUST be derived from a strong source of entropy, and the maskin"}, {"url": "https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-ietf-hybi-thewebsocketprotocol-07", "title": "draft-ietf-hybi-thewebsocketprotocol-07", "for": "The masking key is a 32-bit value chosen at random by the client. [...] The masking key needs to be unpredictable; thus, the masking key MUST be derived from a strong source of entropy, and the maskin"}, {"url": "https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc6455", "title": "RFC 6455: The WebSocket Protocol | RFC Editor", "for": "The masking key for a given frame MUST NOT make it simple for a server/proxy to predict the masking key for a subsequent frame."}, {"url": "https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/open/n3161/xshtext.pdf", "title": "Introduction - Open-std.org", "for": "The masking key for a given frame MUST NOT make it simple for a server/proxy to predict the masking key for a subsequent frame."}], "key_reasons": ["The code generates a single 32-bit mask at connection establishment (Curl_ws_accept line 1340) and reuses it for every frame's header and payload masking (ws_enc_write_head line 879, ws_enc_write_payload line 930).", "RFC 6455 §5.3 explicitly requires a fresh, unpredictable masking key for each frame; the current implementation violates this MUST requirement.", "The mask is transmitted in clear in every frame header, making it trivial for any observer to learn and predict all subsequent masking keys.", "This enables the cache poisoning attacks described in RFC 6455 §10.3, where an attacker can craft WebSocket frames that mimic HTTP requests to poison intermediary caches.", "The flaw exists in both the current master and the latest release (curl-8_15_0), as shown by the provided code for both versions."], "missing_info": [], "reply": "Thank you for this clear and well-documented report. The code confirms that curl generates one masking key at WebSocket connection establishment and reuses it for all frames, violating RFC 6455 §5.3's requirement for a fresh, unpredictable key per frame. Since the mask is sent in clear in every frame header, an attacker can trivially predict all subsequent masking keys, enabling the cache poisoning attacks described in RFC 6455 §10.3. This is a valid security issue affecting all versions with WebSocket support. We will work on a fix that generates a new random mask for each outgoing frame.", "code": [{"name": "lib/ws.c", "start": 1276, "end": 1423, "reason": "lines 1340-1340 cited by the report; definition of Curl_ws_accept", "rows": [{"n": 1276, "text": "CURLcode Curl_ws_accept(struct Curl_easy *data,", "notes": []}, {"n": 1277, "text": "                        const char *mem, size_t nread)", "notes": []}, {"n": 1278, "text": "{", "notes": []}, {"n": 1279, "text": "  struct SingleRequest *k = &data->req;", "notes": []}, {"n": 1280, "text": "  struct websocket *ws;", "notes": []}, {"n": 1281, "text": "  struct Curl_cwriter *ws_dec_writer = NULL;", "notes": []}, {"n": 1282, "text": "  struct Curl_creader *ws_enc_reader = NULL;", "notes": []}, {"n": 1283, "text": "  CURLcode result;", "notes": []}, {"n": 1284, "text": "", "notes": []}, {"n": 1285, "text": "  DEBUGASSERT(data->conn);", "notes": []}, {"n": 1286, "text": "  ws = Curl_conn_meta_get(data->conn, CURL_META_PROTO_WS_CONN);", "notes": []}, {"n": 1287, "text": "  if(!ws) {", "notes": []}, {"n": 1288, "text": "    size_t chunk_size = WS_CHUNK_SIZE;", "notes": []}, {"n": 1289, "text": "    ws = calloc(1, sizeof(*ws));", "notes": []}, {"n": 1290, "text": "    if(!ws)", "notes": []}, {"n": 1291, "text": "      return CURLE_OUT_OF_MEMORY;", "notes": []}, {"n": 1292, "text": "#ifdef DEBUGBUILD", "notes": []}, {"n": 1293, "text": "    {", "notes": []}, {"n": 1294, "text": "      const char *p = getenv(\"CURL_WS_CHUNK_SIZE\");", "notes": []}, {"n": 1295, "text": "      if(p) {", "notes": []}, {"n": 1296, "text": "        curl_off_t l;", "notes": []}, {"n": 1297, "text": "        if(!curlx_str_number(&p, &l, 1*1024*1024))", "notes": []}, {"n": 1298, "text": "          chunk_size = (size_t)l;", "notes": []}, {"n": 1299, "text": "      }", "notes": []}, {"n": 1300, "text": "    }", "notes": []}, {"n": 1301, "text": "#endif", "notes": []}, {"n": 1302, "text": "    CURL_TRC_WS(data, \"WS, using chunk size %zu\", chunk_size);", "n